+tightly packed RGBA8 data. Input channels are straight (unassociated) sRGB;
+alpha is independent coverage. Rows run from visual top to bottom without
+padding. The presenter converts each pixel to premultiplied RGBA8 before
+declaring `PremultipliedLast` to CoreGraphics. Frame dimensions are limited to
+4096 by 4096 (64 MiB), both the exact byte length and allocated `Vec` capacity
+are bounded, and backing scale must be one of the window boundary's supported
+integral scales: 1, 2, or 3 pixels per point.
 
-Presentation copies frame bytes into Core Foundation data, prepares a
-CoreGraphics image, then assigns that retained image to the native view's
-Core Animation layer. The layer owns its current image; replacement is a
-synchronous single-property publication, with the prior image retained by the
-layer until replacement. This is only the frame presentation seam. It does
+Presentation converts the owned pixels in place, copies them into Core
+Foundation data, and fully prepares a CoreGraphics image before changing layer
+properties. CoreGraphics interprets the supplied byte rows from top to bottom;
+the first row remains the visual top, with no orientation transform. The image
+uses the sRGB color space. The provider retains its immutable CFData, and the
+layer retains the published CGImage for display. If a guarded native update
+fails, the presenter restores the prior contents, scale, and gravity while
+retaining the prior image. This is only the frame presentation seam. It does
 not integrate a document model, editor workflow, SVG renderer, or complete V1
 application flow. Package tests do not establish live AppKit rendering on a
 macOS GUI session.
